    {
      "id": "p0145-b005",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Japan uses the metric system, so the translator converted the measurement of \"two inches\" to \"five centimeters\". This is an understandable translation change that hardly seems noteworthy on its own, but it's also a nice example of how math - and not just text - can play a part in the localization process too.",
        "raw": "Japan uses the metric system, so the translator converted the measurement of\n\"two inches\" to \"five centimeters\". This is an understandable translation change\nthat hardly seems noteworthy on its own, but it's also a nice example of how\nmath - and not just text - can play a part in the localization process too.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.08485503,
          0.38140714,
          0.70251931,
          0.4494
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.08281549,
          0.38069286,
          0.7069757,
          0.45047143
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          18,
          19,
          20,
          21
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "日本使用公制，因此译者把“两英寸”换算成了“五厘米”。这个改动很容易理解，单独看似乎没什么值得一提，但它也说明：本地化过程中，除了文字，数学也可能派上用场。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
